 "method": "Pileup parsed as pkg/robocop/utils/getReads.py:188-218: whitespace-split columns; col0 chrom, col1 0-based start, col3 mod code (keep upper()=='A'), col5 strand ('+'=Watson, '-'=Crick), n=int(col9) valid coverage, k=int(col11) modified count. Rows with the same (chrom,start,strand) are summed, as getReads does with +=. Rows are then kept only if the reference base (analysis/inputs/SacCer3.fa via Bio.SeqIO) is A for Watson or T for Crick: robocop.py's fiber emission scores only those positions and zeroes the rest (nucleotide_ref 0/3), so mismatched rows (~30%, mostly n=1) never reach binom.pmf; their count is reported under rows_dropped_ref_base_mismatch. 'Scored base' = one (chrom,pos,strand) with n>0 (n=0 rows are counted separately; they carry no evidence). 1-based pos = col1+1. rDNA = chrXII 1-based 451,435-468,729 inclusive; 'genome_ex_rdna' = all chromosomes in the file (incl. chrM) minus rDNA. Runs: per position keep the strand with larger n (Watson on tie); a run is a maximal stretch of consecutive positions each with a scored base. A 4-window is any 4 consecutive positions inside a run (overlapping windows all counted); its min_n is the min of the 4 n values; 'kn>=0.8' requires all four k/n>=0.8. 'runs_with' counts maximal runs containing >=1 qualifying window. Underflow: scipy.stats.binom.pmf(k,n,p)==0.0 in float64 for p=0.0242 or p=0.2647 (either one => at least one state floored to 1e-30). Quantiles use numpy linear interpolation; n histogram bins are [lo,hi) with a final [10000,inf).",
